.TH KEYS 6
.SH NAME
keys \- chords the draw server acts on
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Intuition (4)
reads
.B $home/lib/keys
first, or
.B /lib/keys
when there is none, at start and again on a
.B reload
line written to
.BR /srv/draw/ctl .
The file names
.IR chords :
a key held down together with alt, control or shift, caught before any
window sees it, and what each one does.
Alt is the modifier most lines use, the Amiga key on this look.
.PP
Each line is a chord, some space, and an action:
.IP
.EX
chord   action argument ...
.EE
.PP
A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line, wherever it falls, and
a blank line is skipped.
A line whose first word does not parse as a chord, or that names no
action after it, is skipped as well.
At most sixty-four chords are kept; a file with more is read only that
far.
.PP
A
.I chord
is one or more modifier names, each followed by a hyphen, and then a key:
.IP
.EX
alt\-key
ctrl\-key   (or control\-key)
shift\-key
mod\-mod\-key
.EE
.PP
The
.I key
is a single character, taken without regard to shift, or one of the
names
.BR tab ", " space ", " esc ", " del ", " up ", " down ", " left ", "
.BR right ", " home ", " end ", or " f1
through
.BR f12 .
A letter is matched without regard to case, and a digit typed with shift
held (the symbol above it on a PC keyboard) is matched by the digit's own
key.
.PP
A key held with a modifier this file does not name reaches the window in
front like an ordinary key, which is what lets a program bind one of its
own.
.PP
The action is everything after the chord, up to sixty-four bytes of it.
.IR Intuition (4)
knows these actions itself and does them without help:
.TP 20
.B close
hang up the window in front
.TP
.B cycle
move the focus to the next window on the current workspace
.TP
.B zoom
fill the screen below the bar with the window in front, or return it to
where it was
.TP
.B back
send the window in front to the back
.TP
.BI "move " "dx dy"
move the window in front by
.I dx , dy
pixels
.TP
.BI "workspace " n
switch to workspace
.I n
.TP
.B next
.TP
.B prev
switch to the next or previous workspace, wrapping past nine back to one
.TP
.BI "send " n
move the window in front to workspace
.I n
.TP
.B overview
open or close the picture of every workspace at once
.PD
.PP
An action this list does not name is not an error.
It is sent, verbatim and unaltered, as one line on
.BR /srv/draw/hotkey ,
which the desktop reads and carries out; a chord bound to
.B window rc -i
or
.B menu
means whatever the desktop program listening on
.B hotkey
takes those words to mean.
.SH EXAMPLES
Lines from the file the tree ships:
.IP
.EX
alt\-w        close                 # the window in front
alt\-tab      cycle                 # the focus to the next window
alt\-up       move 0 \-16            # nudge the window in front
alt\-1        workspace 1
alt\-shift\-1  send 1                # the window in front, to workspace 1
alt\-space    overview              # every workspace at once
alt\-n        window rc \-i          # a new shell, which the desktop runs
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.B $home/lib/keys
a user's own chords, read first
.TP
.B /lib/keys
the machine's, read when there is no user file
.TP
.B /srv/draw/hotkey
where an action this file does not know is sent
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/intuition/keys.odin
.br
.B servers/intuition/keys
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intuition (4),
.IR workspaces (6),
.IR kbdfs (4)
.SH BUGS
A malformed chord or a line with no action is silently skipped rather than
reported; there is no way to ask
.IR intuition (4)
which lines it kept.
